      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Activation of coronary vascular α-adrenoceptors results in vasoconstriction which competes with metabolic vasodilation during sympathetic activation. Epicardial conduit vessel constriction is largely mediated by α(1)-adrenoceptors; the constriction of the resistive microcirculation largely by α(2)-adrenoceptors, but also by α(1)-adrenoceptors. There is no firm evidence that α-adrenergic coronary vasoconstriction exerts a beneficial effect on transmural blood flow distribution. In fact, α-blockade in anesthetized and conscious dogs improves blood flow to all transmural layers, during normoperfusion and hypoperfusion. Also, in patients with coronary artery disease, blockade of α(1)- and α(2)-adrenoceptors improves coronary blood flow, myocardial function and metabolism."
      },
